      "text": "foam fractionation ( Buckley et al., 2023 ) and low-temperature miner - alization ( Trang et al., 2022 ) have also been successful. It can be concluded from the literature that while much effort is being made to develop PFAS removal techniques, little attention has been given to the impacts of PFAS on conventional drinking water treatment processes so far. Algae, as primary producers in natural waters, can be influenced by PFAS in surface water and thereby affect water treatment performance. For instance, the metabolism of algae can be impacted by PFAS, and organic matter released from broken algae will affect water quality ( Aziz et al., 2022 ; Li et al., 2012 ). Previous research on PFAS and algae suggest that PFAS has a toxic effect on algae ( Lata \u0142 a et al., 2009 ; Liu et al., 2022 ; Niu et al., 2019 ; Zhao et al., 2023 ); however, much higher concentra - tions of algae or PFAS than those observed in real water were considered in these studies. In view of this, the underlying influences of PFAS and algae on DWTP processes (e.g. coagulation, sand and membrane filtra - tion) remain unknown to date. In this work, we evaluated PFAS effects on typical treatment pro -"
